<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Dalton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Dalton was an English chemist, physicist and meteorologist whose work laid the foundations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a>. Born into a Quaker family in Eaglesfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> he settled in Manchester and joined its Literary and Philosophical Society in 1794. He proposed that each element consists of identical atoms of characteristic weight, which combine in fixed whole-number proportions, and set out the model in A New System of Chemical Philosophy (1808). He kept daily weather records for over fifty years, formulated the law of partial pressures and gave the first scientific description of colour blindness, still called Daltonism in several languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-dalton.jpg" alt="John Dalton" width="330" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Phillips · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Dalton_by_Thomas_Phillips,_1835.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Dalton was born on 5 or 6 September 1766 into a Quaker family in Eaglesfield, near Cockermouth, the son of a weaver. He was taught by his father and by John Fletcher, who ran a Quaker school at Pardshaw Hall. The family was too poor to support him for long, and from the age of ten he earned his living in the service of the local Quaker Elihu Robinson, a competent meteorologist who interested him in mathematics and meteorology. In 1787, aged 21, he began a meteorological diary that over 57 years held more than 200,000 observations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1794, soon after reaching Manchester, he joined the Manchester Literary and Philosophical Society and presented a paper on the vision of colours. Since he and his brother were both colour blind, he saw that the condition must be hereditary. In 1800 he became the society's secretary, and in October 1801 he presented four essays on mixed gases and steam. His paper on the absorption of gases by water, read on 21 October 1803, contained the law of partial pressures now known as Dalton's law.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a> is arguably his most important work, though its origin is not fully understood. William Higgins claimed from 1814 to 1819 that Dalton had plagiarised him, but the essential novelty was a method of calculating relative atomic weights, and Dalton's priority for that is uncontested. His first table gave six elements relative to hydrogen as 1. To fix formulas he used a controversial rule that atoms combine in the simplest possible way, which led him to give water as OH but to the correct formulas for the two oxides of carbon. A New System of Chemical Philosophy listed 21 elements and 17 simple molecules.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dalton never married and for his last 26 years lived in a room in the Manchester home of the Rev. W. Johns. He was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1822. After strokes in 1837, 1838 and May 1844 he recorded his last weather observation on 26 July and was found dead on 27 July. His body lay in state in Manchester Town Hall for four days, and more than 40,000 people filed past his coffin. The unit of mass called the dalton is named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Dalton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
